# Python standards

## Project and dependencies

- Every Python project is a uv project with a `pyproject.toml`. Add packages with
  `uv add`, dev tools with `uv add --dev`. Run everything through `uv run`.
- Ruff is the formatter and linter; its config lives in `pyproject.toml`.
- Target the Python version pinned in `pyproject.toml`; use its modern syntax
  (`X | None`, `list[str]`, `match` where it reads better).

## APIs

- HTTP APIs are FastAPI. One `APIRouter` per resource under `app/api/routes/`.
- Request and response bodies are Pydantic models, never bare dicts.
- Route functions stay thin: parse, call a service function, return. Logic lives
  in `app/services/`, I/O in `app/clients/` or `app/repositories/`.
- Inject dependencies with `Depends`; do not import global clients into routes.

## Configuration

- All settings are read in one module, `app/core/config.py`, using
  `pydantic-settings` (`BaseSettings`, `env_file=".env"`). Nothing else calls
  `os.environ` or `os.getenv`.
- Every setting has an entry in `.env.example` with a safe placeholder and a
  one-line comment. Adding a setting means updating both files in one change.

## Readability

- Type hints on every function signature. No `Any` without a comment saying why.
- Names say what a thing is; no abbreviations beyond the domain's own.
- Functions do one thing and fit on a screen. Prefer early returns to nesting.
- Docstrings state intent and non-obvious constraints, not a restatement of the code.
- Raise specific exceptions. Never `except Exception: pass`.
- Use `logging` (structured where available), never `print`, in application code.
- Async all the way down in request paths: no blocking calls inside `async def`.
